<title>USA: S&amp;P cuts stocks in tax-advantage asset allocation.</title>
<headline>S&amp;P cuts stocks in tax-advantage asset allocation.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-02-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Standard &amp; Poor's said it lowered the stock portion of its tax-advantaged accounts to 50 percent from 55 percent and raised cash to 20 percent from 15 percent.</p>
<p>Bonds were kept at 30 percent of the portfolio.</p>
<p>Noting that the S&amp;P 500 index is up 76 percent in a little more than two years, S&amp;P said in the February 19 issue of its weekly Outlook news, &quot;There's little question, however, that the market is now overvalued. Watching carefully for signs of deterioration is critical.&quot;</p>
<p>Standard &amp; Poor's is a unit of McGraw-Hill Cos.</p>
